      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so i am stuck on an iam policy issue. i have a lambda that needs to write logs to an s3 bucket but i keep getting access denied errors in the cloudwatch logs even though i think the policy is right. can you check this json for me?\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\",\n        \"s3:GetBucketLocation\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your IAM policy looks mostly correct for allowing `PutObject` to your S3 bucket, which is what you need for writing logs. However, when you're dealing with permissions, it's often a matter of the *least* amount of permissions needed, and sometimes the access denial is due to a missing bucket-level policy or a service role issue, not just this policy. You might also need `s3:ListBucket` if the Lambda is trying to list contents before writing, though `PutObject` is the main action here. I'd check if the Lambda's execution role has this policy attached, and if the S3 bucket policy explicitly allows access from that role.",
